#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
copy-user-lint - FAILS THE BUILD if a syscall handler DIRECTLY DEREFERENCES a
user pointer (reads/writes *p, p->field, p[i], or hands p to a bare
memcpy/memset/...) instead of going through the #509 TOCTOU-safe copy_*_user
primitives - unless the handler is on the checked-in exemption/known-gap
manifest (kernel/syscall-copyuser.manifest).

WHY THIS EXISTS (#566, the adoption-enforcement half of #509 / #514)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
#509 made copy_from_user/copy_to_user/strncpy_from_user/clear_user an ATOMIC
check-AND-use: the entry U/S walk plus a fault-fixup in mm/fault.c, so if a
sibling thread unmaps/remaps the page between the check and the use, the copy
faults to a fixup and returns -EFAULT instead of the kernel dereferencing a
freed/remapped frame. The OLD pattern - validate_user_ptr() at entry, THEN a
plain `*p` / memcpy(p,...) deeper in the handler - is a TOCTOU hole: the check
and the use are two separate events and the page can change in between.

sibling gate syscall-ptr-lint (#565) proves each pointer syscall is VALIDATED
AT ENTRY. That is necessary but NOT sufficient: entry validation is exactly the
check half of the TOCTOU pair. This gate proves the USE half is atomic too -
that the handler does not then direct-deref the pointer it just validated.

SCOPE (stated plainly, because a gate's blind spots must be documented)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
This lint reasons about THE SYSCALL LAYER: the dispatcher case bodies in
proc/syscall.c AND the handler functions a pointer-syscall case calls with a
user pointer (one level of tracing). A direct deref of a user pointer inside
that layer - a `st->st_mode = ...` on a `(k_stat_t*)ubuf`, a
`memcpy(kbuf, userptr, n)` - is the unit it catches and gates. A user pointer
that a handler hands DOWN to a subsystem in another translation unit (VFS,
net/*_kcr3, crypto) leaves the layer; the deref, if any, is that subsystem's
contract and out of this lint's scope (many switch CR3 or bounce internally).
The boundary is therefore: "the syscall layer must not itself raw-deref a user
pointer; bounce it through copy_*_user first, or hand it to a subsystem." That
line is enforceable without whole-tree dataflow and is where struct in/out
params (the classic TOCTOU targets) actually live.

THE SYSCALL LAYER IS NOT ONE FILE ANY MORE (#746b), AND THAT IS WHY --handlers
EXISTS. Until 2026-08-08 the analysed set was literally "functions defined in
proc/syscall.c", so moving a handler to a sibling translation unit removed it
from this lint's sight WITHOUT FAILING ANYTHING. That was measured, not feared:
with the legacy fd layer deleted from syscall.c, this lint went from reporting
`DEBT SYS_READ` to reporting `SYS_READ ... no longer has an unsafe direct deref
(adopted?); remove it (stale)` and exited 0. The tree read CLEANER than before
while being no safer, and the note actively invited deletion of the ledger entry
recording a real unfixed TOCTOU hole. Four pointer syscalls left the analysed
set (SYS_OPEN, SYS_READ, SYS_WRITE, SYS_READDIR); three of them would have gone
from passing GENUINELY to passing VACUOUSLY, and nothing in the output could
distinguish the two.

So every file that defines syscall handlers must be named with --handlers, and:
  * a --handlers path that does not exist is FATAL, because a typo would shrink
    the analysed set silently, which is the very fault being fixed;
  * the analysed set is PRINTED on every green run, so the scope is visible
    rather than assumed (#514: a gate must report what it looked at).
--self-test proves the tracing actually crosses the file boundary, by showing
the SAME injected violation GREEN without --handlers and RED with it.

HOW IT ENUMERATES (auto-discovery, not a hand list; self-updating)
------------------------------------------------------------------
1. Parse the dispatcher switch. A `case SYS_X:` that casts an argN to a pointer
   is a POINTER SYSCALL (same discovery as syscall-ptr-lint; fallthrough labels
   share the following body). This is self-updating: a new pointer syscall is
   seen the moment its case is added.
2. For each pointer syscall, build its ANALYSIS TEXT = the case body PLUS the
   body of every function defined in syscall.c that the case calls and passes a
   user pointer to (the handler). Inside a handler, the user pointers are its
   pointer PARAMETERS plus simple local aliases (`T *v = (T*)param;`).
3. In the analysis text, a DIRECT DEREF of a user pointer v is any of:
      *v            v->field        v[i]
      memcpy/memmove/memset/strcpy/strncpy/strcat/sprintf/snprintf(... v ...)
   A deref routed through copy_from_user / copy_to_user / strncpy_from_user /
   strnlen_user / clear_user / __uaccess_* is SAFE (adopted), not a direct deref.

THE RULES (same shape as syscall-ptr-lint)
------------------------------------------
For every pointer syscall SYS_X whose analysis text has an unsafe direct deref:
  * on [EXEMPT] in the manifest  => the handler is already safe for a documented
    reason (bounces once through a kernel buffer via a bare copy the regex
    cannot model, or has legitimate kernel-pointer callers). Good.
  * on [KNOWN_GAP] in the manifest => a DEBT-LEDGER entry: a real still-direct
    deref, TRACKED and printed LOUDLY every build but tolerated (build not
    wedged) until converted. NOT a silent exemption.
  * else => the build FAILS, naming SYS_X and the deref. Convert it to
    copy_*_user, or (if genuinely already safe) add it under [EXEMPT] with the
    reason, or (if honestly deferred) under [KNOWN_GAP] with the owning task.

A pointer syscall with NO unsafe direct deref (pure delegation, or every deref
via copy_*_user) is ADOPTED - clean, needs no manifest entry.

Manifest hygiene (typo guard): a name under [EXEMPT]/[KNOWN_GAP] that is not a
real SYS_* #define fails the build. A name that no longer direct-derefs is a
stale entry: reported as an advisory to remove it (non-fatal).

USAGE
  copy-user-lint --header <syscall.h> --dispatch <syscall.c> \
                 --manifest <syscall-copyuser.manifest>
  copy-user-lint ... --self-test   # RED on a direct-deref fixture, GREEN on the real tree
"""
import argparse, os, re, sys, tempfile

DEFINE_RE = re.compile(r'^\s*#\s*define\s+(SYS_[A-Z0-9_]+)\s+(\d+)', re.M)
LABEL_RE  = re.compile(r'\bcase\s+(SYS_[A-Z0-9_]+)\s*:|\bdefault\s*:')
PTR_CAST_RE = re.compile(
    r'\(\s*(?:const\s+|volatile\s+|struct\s+|unsigned\s+|signed\s+)*'
    r'[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:\s+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)*\s*\*+\s*\)\s*arg([1-6])')
# a local bound to an argN pointer cast:  T *v = (T *)argN;   or  v = (T*)argN
CAST_BIND_RE = re.compile(
    r'\b([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*=\s*\(\s*(?:const\s+|volatile\s+|struct\s+|unsigned\s+|signed\s+)*'
    r'[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*(?:\s+[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_]*)*\s*\*+\s*\)\s*(arg[1-6]|[A-Za-z_]\w*)')
CALL_RE   = re.compile(r'\b([a-z_][a-z0-9_]*)\s*\(')
SAFE_FUNCS = ('copy_from_user', 'copy_to_user', 'strncpy_from_user',
              'strnlen_user', 'clear_user')
DEREF_FUNCS = ('memcpy', 'memmove', 'memset', 'strcpy', 'strncpy', 'strcat',
               'strncat', 'sprintf', 'snprintf', 'strlcpy')
# words that can precede `*v` yet are NOT a type (so `*v` is a real deref there)
_KEYWORDS = {'return', 'if', 'while', 'for', 'else', 'do', 'switch', 'case',
             'sizeof', 'goto'}

# a top-level function definition:  <ret ...> name(<params>) {   (column 0 start)
FUNC_DEF_RE = re.compile(
    r'^([A-Za-z_][\w\s\*]*?\b)([a-z_][a-z0-9_]+)\s*\(([^;{}]*)\)\s*\{', re.M)


def read(path):
    with open(path, 'r') as f:
        return f.read()


def strip_comments(s):
    s = re.sub(r'/\*.*?\*/', '', s, flags=re.S)
    s = re.sub(r'//[^\n]*', '', s)
    return s


def parse_defines(header_src):
    name2num, num2name = {}, {}
    for m in DEFINE_RE.finditer(header_src):
        name2num[m.group(1)] = int(m.group(2))
        num2name.setdefault(int(m.group(2)), m.group(1))
    return name2num, num2name


def _match_block(src, open_idx):
    """Given index of a '{', return index just past the matching '}'."""
    depth = 0
    i = open_idx
    n = len(src)
    while i < n:
        c = src[i]
        if c == '{':
            depth += 1
        elif c == '}':
            depth -= 1
            if depth == 0:
                return i + 1
        i += 1
    return n


def parse_functions(dispatch_nc):
    """Map function-name -> (param_str, body_text) for every top-level function
    defined in the (comment-stripped) file."""
    funcs = {}
    for m in FUNC_DEF_RE.finditer(dispatch_nc):
        name = m.group(2)
        if name in ('if', 'for', 'while', 'switch', 'return', 'sizeof', 'case'):
            continue
        params = m.group(3)
        brace = dispatch_nc.index('{', m.end() - 1)
        end = _match_block(dispatch_nc, brace)
        funcs[name] = (params, dispatch_nc[brace:end])
    return funcs


def parse_pointer_cases(dispatch_nc):
    """Return {SYS_X: casebody_text} for every case whose body casts an arg to a
    pointer. Fallthrough labels share the following body."""
    try:
        body = dispatch_nc[dispatch_nc.index('switch (num)'):]
    except ValueError:
        body = dispatch_nc
    labels = [(m.start(), m.end(), (m.group(1) or '__default__'))
              for m in LABEL_RE.finditer(body)]
    out, pending = {}, []
    for i, (s, e, name) in enumerate(labels):
        nxt = labels[i + 1][0] if i + 1 < len(labels) else len(body)
        seg = body[e:nxt]
        pending.append(name)
        if seg.strip():
            if PTR_CAST_RE.search(seg):
                for nm in pending:
                    if nm != '__default__':
                        out[nm] = seg
            pending = []
    return out


def pointer_params(param_str):
    """Names of pointer-typed parameters (they carry user pointers into a
    syscall handler). `char *p` / `void *q` / `const T *r` / `T **s`."""
    names = []
    for part in param_str.split(','):
        part = part.strip()
        if not part or part == 'void':
            continue
        if '*' in part:
            m = re.search(r'\*\s*([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*$', part)
            if m:
                names.append(m.group(1))
    return names


def user_ptr_names(text, seeds):
    """Given analysis text and seed user-pointer names, expand with simple
    aliases `x = (T*)seed;` / `x = seed;` to a fixpoint."""
    names = set(seeds)
    for _ in range(6):
        added = False
        for m in CAST_BIND_RE.finditer(text):
            lhs, rhs = m.group(1), m.group(2)
            if (rhs in names or rhs.startswith('arg')) and lhs not in names:
                names.add(lhs); added = True
        # plain alias:  x = seed;
        for m in re.finditer(r'\b([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*=\s*([A-Za-z_]\w*)\s*[;,)]', text):
            lhs, rhs = m.group(1), m.group(2)
            if rhs in names and lhs not in names and lhs not in ('if', 'return'):
                names.add(lhs); added = True
        if not added:
            break
    return names


def find_derefs(text, unames):
    """Return a list of (kind, snippet) for each UNSAFE direct deref of a user
    pointer in text. A deref inside a copy_*_user(...) call is SAFE."""
    # Blank out safe-copy call argument lists so a user ptr appearing as their
    # argument is not counted as a direct deref.
    safe_spans = []
    for fn in SAFE_FUNCS:
        for m in re.finditer(r'\b' + fn + r'\s*\(', text):
            op = text.index('(', m.end() - 1)
            depth = 0; i = op
            while i < len(text):
                if text[i] == '(':
                    depth += 1
                elif text[i] == ')':
                    depth -= 1
                    if depth == 0:
                        break
                i += 1
            safe_spans.append((m.start(), i + 1))

    def in_safe(pos):
        return any(a <= pos < b for a, b in safe_spans)

    hits = []
    for v in sorted(unames, key=len, reverse=True):
        ve = re.escape(v)
        # v->field   and   v[i]
        for pat, kind in ((r'\b' + ve + r'\s*->', 'v->field'),
                          (r'\b' + ve + r'\s*\[', 'v[i]')):
            for m in re.finditer(pat, text):
                if not in_safe(m.start()):
                    hits.append((kind, text[max(0, m.start()-8):m.start()+18].strip()))
        # *v  scalar deref (read or write). EXCLUDE the declaration `T *v = ...`:
        # a declaration has a TYPE IDENTIFIER right before the `*` (e.g.
        # `char *ub`, `unsigned long *up`); a deref has punctuation / an operator
        # / `=` / a keyword (`if (t) *t = x`, `return *p`, `; *p; p++`).
        for m in re.finditer(r'\*\s*' + ve + r'\b', text):
            if in_safe(m.start()):
                continue
            pre = text[:m.start()].rstrip(' \t*')
            mw = re.search(r'([A-Za-z_]\w*)$', pre)
            if mw and mw.group(1) not in _KEYWORDS:
                continue  # `type *v` -> a declaration, not a deref
            hits.append(('*v', text[m.start():m.start()+18].strip()))
    # bare mem/str funcs taking a user pointer as an argument
    for fn in DEREF_FUNCS:
        for m in re.finditer(r'\b' + fn + r'\s*\(', text):
            op = text.index('(', m.end() - 1)
            depth = 0; i = op
            while i < len(text):
                if text[i] == '(':
                    depth += 1
                elif text[i] == ')':
                    depth -= 1
                    if depth == 0:
                        break
                i += 1
            args = text[op + 1:i]
            toks = set(re.findall(r'[A-Za-z_]\w*', args))
            if toks & unames:
                hits.append((fn + '()', (fn + '(' + args.strip())[:40]))
    return hits


def analyze_case(sys_name, casebody, funcs):
    """Return list of unsafe derefs for a pointer syscall: case body + one level
    of in-file handler bodies it passes a user pointer to."""
    text = casebody
    seeds = set()
    # user pointers named directly in the case body
    for m in CAST_BIND_RE.finditer(casebody):
        seeds.add(m.group(1))
    unames = user_ptr_names(casebody, seeds)
    hits = find_derefs(casebody, unames | {'__inlinecast__'})

    # trace one level: any in-file function this case calls that takes a pointer
    # param inherits its pointer params as user pointers.
    for m in CALL_RE.finditer(casebody):
        fn = m.group(1)
        if fn in funcs and fn not in SAFE_FUNCS and fn not in DEREF_FUNCS:
            params, body = funcs[fn]
            pp = pointer_params(params)
            if not pp:
                continue
            hunames = user_ptr_names(body, pp)
            hits += find_derefs(body, hunames)
    return hits


def parse_manifest(path):
    exempt, gap = [], []
    if not os.path.exists(path):
        return exempt, gap
    section = None
    for raw in read(path).splitlines():
        line = raw.split('#', 1)[0].rstrip()
        st = line.strip()
        if not st:
            continue
        up = st.upper()
        if up == '[EXEMPT]':
            section = exempt; continue
        if up == '[KNOWN_GAP]':
            section = gap; continue
        parts = st.split(None, 1)
        name = parts[0]
        reason = parts[1] if len(parts) > 1 else ''
        if section is None:
            section = exempt
        section.append((name, reason))
    return exempt, gap


def run(header, dispatch, manifest, handlers=(), out=sys.stdout):
    name2num, _ = parse_defines(read(header))
    disp_nc = strip_comments(read(dispatch))
    funcs = parse_functions(disp_nc)
    ptr_cases = parse_pointer_cases(disp_nc)

    # #746b: handler bodies that live in a SIBLING translation unit. A missing
    # path is FATAL: a typo that silently shrank the analysed set would be the
    # exact fault this option exists to prevent (see the SCOPE note above).
    for hpath in handlers:
        if not os.path.exists(hpath):
            print("copy-user-lint: FAIL - --handlers source not found: %s"
                  % hpath, file=out)
            print("   A handler source that cannot be read is not coverage;"
                  " it is a gap that looks like coverage.", file=out)
            return 1
        for nm, val in parse_functions(strip_comments(read(hpath))).items():
            funcs.setdefault(nm, val)   # the dispatch file wins a name collision
    exempt, gap = parse_manifest(manifest)
    exempt_names = {n for n, _ in exempt}
    gap_names = {n for n, _ in gap}

    def p(*a):
        print(*a, file=out)

    if not ptr_cases:
        p("copy-user-lint: ERROR - found NO pointer syscalls in %s." % dispatch)
        p("   The dispatcher parse failed; a lint that checks nothing is worse")
        p("   than no lint. Refusing to pass.")
        return 1

    deref = {}  # SYS_X -> hits (only those with >=1 unsafe direct deref)
    for nm, seg in ptr_cases.items():
        hits = analyze_case(nm, seg, funcs)
        if hits:
            deref[nm] = hits

    deref_names = sorted(deref.keys(), key=lambda n: name2num.get(n, 9999))

    ex, gp, undeclared = [], [], []
    for n in deref_names:
        if n in exempt_names:
            ex.append(n)
        elif n in gap_names:
            gp.append(n)
        else:
            undeclared.append(n)

    fatal = False
    # manifest hygiene: bad names
    bad_names = [n for n in (exempt_names | gap_names) if n not in name2num]
    if bad_names:
        fatal = True
        p("copy-user-lint: FAIL - manifest names that are not SYS_* #defines")
        p("   (typo? a mis-spelled allowlist entry protects nothing):")
        for n in sorted(bad_names):
            p("   BAD NAME: %s" % n)
    # stale: on manifest but no longer direct-derefs
    stale = [n for n in (exempt_names | gap_names)
             if n in name2num and n not in deref]
    for n in stale:
        p("copy-user-lint: note - %s is on the allowlist but no longer has an"
          " unsafe direct deref (adopted?); remove it (stale)." % n)

    if gp:
        p("")
        p("copy-user-lint: KNOWN GAP LEDGER - %d syscall handler(s) still"
          " DIRECT-DEREF a user pointer (TOCTOU debt, NOT silently exempt):" % len(gp))
        greason = dict(gap)
        for n in sorted(gp, key=lambda x: name2num.get(x, 9999)):
            kinds = sorted({k for k, _ in deref[n]})
            p("   DEBT %-22s derefs=%s  %s"
              % (n, ','.join(kinds), greason.get(n, '')))
        p("   These are real TOCTOU holes to close (bounce through copy_*_user).")
        p("   They do NOT fail the build, but they are on the record every build.")

    if undeclared:
        fatal = True
        p("")
        p("copy-user-lint: FAIL - %d syscall handler(s) DIRECT-DEREF a user"
          " pointer with no copy_*_user and no manifest entry:" % len(undeclared))
        for n in sorted(undeclared, key=lambda x: name2num.get(x, 9999)):
            kinds = sorted({k for k, _ in deref[n]})
            ex1 = deref[n][0][1]
            p("   DIRECT-DEREF: %s (num %s) derefs=%s  e.g. `%s`"
              % (n, name2num.get(n, '?'), ','.join(kinds), ex1))
        p("")
        p("   A user pointer dereferenced directly after an entry check is a")
        p("   TOCTOU hole (#509): a sibling thread can remap the page between the")
        p("   check and the deref. FIX: bounce it through a kernel buffer with")
        p("   copy_from_user / copy_to_user (atomic check-and-use), OR - if the")
        p("   handler is already safe - add it under [EXEMPT] in")
        p("   %s naming why, OR - if honestly deferred -" % os.path.basename(manifest))
        p("   under [KNOWN_GAP] with the owning task.")

    if fatal:
        return 1

    p("")
    p("copy-user-lint: OK - %d pointer syscall(s): %d direct-deref handler(s)"
      " (%d in-handler EXEMPT, %d tracked KNOWN_GAP), rest adopted/delegated."
      " No undeclared TOCTOU holes."
      % (len(ptr_cases), len(deref), len(ex), len(gp)))
    if handlers:
        p("copy-user-lint: handler sources analysed with %s: %s"
          % (os.path.basename(dispatch),
             ', '.join(os.path.basename(h) for h in handlers)))
    return 0


def self_test(header, dispatch, manifest, handlers=()):
    import io
    ok_all = True

    buf = io.StringIO()
    rc = run(header, dispatch, manifest, handlers=handlers, out=buf)
    green_ok = (rc == 0)
    print("self-test GREEN (real tree): rc=%d -> %s"
          % (rc, "PASS" if green_ok else "FAIL (expected 0)"))
    if not green_ok:
        print(buf.getvalue())
    ok_all = ok_all and green_ok

    FAKE = "SYS_COPYUSER_SELFTEST_VICTIM"
    name2num, _ = parse_defines(read(header))
    fake_num = max(name2num.values()) + 4243
    hdr_src = read(header) + "\n#define %s %d\n" % (FAKE, fake_num)
    disp_src = read(dispatch)
    # a dispatcher case that casts arg1 to a pointer and WRITES through it
    inject = ("\n        case %s: {\n"
              "            int *victim = (int *)arg1;\n"
              "            victim[0] = 42;   /* raw user-pointer write */\n"
              "            return 0;\n"
              "        }\n" % FAKE)
    marker = "    switch (num) {"
    idx = disp_src.index(marker) + len(marker)
    disp_src = disp_src[:idx] + inject + disp_src[idx:]

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as td:
        hp = os.path.join(td, "h.h"); dp = os.path.join(td, "d.c")
        with open(hp, 'w') as f: f.write(hdr_src)
        with open(dp, 'w') as f: f.write(disp_src)
        buf = io.StringIO()
        rc = run(hp, dp, manifest, handlers=handlers, out=buf)
        text = buf.getvalue()
        red_ok = (rc != 0) and (FAKE in text) and ("DIRECT-DEREF" in text)
        print("self-test RED (injected direct-deref handler): rc=%d -> %s"
              % (rc, "PASS" if red_ok else "FAIL (expected non-zero naming the victim)"))
        if not red_ok:
            print(text)
        else:
            for line in text.splitlines():
                if FAKE in line:
                    print("   proof:%s" % line.strip())
        ok_all = ok_all and red_ok

    # ---- #746b: the CROSS-FILE arm ------------------------------------------
    # The point of --handlers is that a handler in a SIBLING file is still
    # analysed. Proving only the RED half would not show the option is what does
    # the work, so the SAME violation is run BOTH ways: blind without
    # --handlers (which is the coverage loss, reproduced on demand) and caught
    # with it. A gate whose new option is untested is a gate with a new hole.
    FAKE2 = "SYS_COPYUSER_SELFTEST_XFILE"
    xnum = max(name2num.values()) + 4244
    xhdr = read(header) + "\n#define %s %d\n" % (FAKE2, xnum)
    xinject = ("\n        case %s: {\n"
               "            char *xv = (char *)arg1;\n"
               "            cul_selftest_xfile_handler(xv);\n"
               "            return 0;\n"
               "        }\n" % FAKE2)
    xdisp = read(dispatch)
    xi = xdisp.index(marker) + len(marker)
    xdisp = xdisp[:xi] + xinject + xdisp[xi:]
    xhandler = ("void cul_selftest_xfile_handler(char *v) {\n"
                "    v[0] = 42;   /* raw user-pointer write, in ANOTHER file */\n"
                "}\n")

    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() as td:
        hp = os.path.join(td, "h.h"); dp = os.path.join(td, "d.c")
        xp = os.path.join(td, "handlers.c")
        with open(hp, 'w') as f: f.write(xhdr)
        with open(dp, 'w') as f: f.write(xdisp)
        with open(xp, 'w') as f: f.write(xhandler)

        buf = io.StringIO()
        rc_blind = run(hp, dp, manifest, handlers=(), out=buf)
        blind_text = buf.getvalue()
        blind_ok = (rc_blind == 0) and (FAKE2 not in blind_text)
        print("self-test BLIND (handler in a sibling file, --handlers NOT given):"
              " rc=%d -> %s" % (rc_blind,
              "PASS (loss reproduced)" if blind_ok else "FAIL (expected 0 and silent)"))
        if not blind_ok:
            print(blind_text)
        ok_all = ok_all and blind_ok

        buf = io.StringIO()
        rc_seen = run(hp, dp, manifest, handlers=(xp,), out=buf)
        seen_text = buf.getvalue()
        seen_ok = (rc_seen != 0) and (FAKE2 in seen_text) and ("DIRECT-DEREF" in seen_text)
        print("self-test RED (same handler, --handlers given): rc=%d -> %s"
              % (rc_seen, "PASS" if seen_ok else "FAIL (expected non-zero naming the victim)"))
        if not seen_ok:
            print(seen_text)
        else:
            for line in seen_text.splitlines():
                if FAKE2 in line:
                    print("   proof:%s" % line.strip())
        ok_all = ok_all and seen_ok

        buf = io.StringIO()
        rc_missing = run(hp, dp, manifest,
                         handlers=(os.path.join(td, "nope.c"),), out=buf)
        miss_ok = (rc_missing != 0) and ("not found" in buf.getvalue())
        print("self-test RED (--handlers path that does not exist): rc=%d -> %s"
              % (rc_missing, "PASS" if miss_ok else "FAIL (expected non-zero)"))
        if not miss_ok:
            print(buf.getvalue())
        ok_all = ok_all and miss_ok

    print("")
    if ok_all:
        print("copy-user-lint --self-test: PASS (RED on direct-deref in the"
              " dispatch file AND in a --handlers file, BLIND without"
              " --handlers, RED on a missing --handlers path, GREEN on real tree)")
        return 0
    print("copy-user-lint --self-test: FAIL - the gate is not trustworthy")
    return 1


def main():
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    ap.add_argument('--header', required=True)
    ap.add_argument('--dispatch', required=True)
    ap.add_argument('--manifest', required=True)
    ap.add_argument('--handlers', action='append', default=[],
                    help='additional C source defining syscall handlers; '
                         'repeatable. A path that does not exist is fatal.')
    ap.add_argument('--self-test', action='store_true')
    a = ap.parse_args()
    if a.self_test:
        return self_test(a.header, a.dispatch, a.manifest, tuple(a.handlers))
    return run(a.header, a.dispatch, a.manifest, tuple(a.handlers))


if __name__ == '__main__':
    sys.exit(main())
